Z||000026||MinnGreenGT||10-23-2001||05:27 PM||||I want it, and I'm even (reasonably) close... I just don't have the cash for it at the moment!  Damnit.  Wanna make a deal for anything?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000027||MinnGreenGT||10-23-2001||05:29 PM||||Rockcrawl... how much to have you do the install on one of these anyway?  <P>BTW: are you still making the extractor hood vents? I want one of those too!||||reg||1
Z||000028||jimmybpei||10-23-2001||06:30 PM||||As for the install of the caddy motors on a scale of 1-10 it is about in my mind an 3.<BR>Very very easy to do and no time at all. i've got about 25 hours into mine and rumming<BR>thanks daleford for jons number. Thanks especilly to rockcrawl, he was lots of help because i am one of the people that he has sent the bypass module to. <P>Bubba joes mounts are pretty good and seems to be the simpilist way to mount it unless someone else comes up with a better idea. the only thing i think i may improve on from the advice of daleford mech. is to install adogbone. <P>Dale what you were going to install is just between us as for the young fella and his father that were going to install a 3800 but changed thir mind to a caddy i'm told after seeing mine. if you need any help just stop by the petro i'll let you copy rockcrawls module  if thats okwith rock.<P>Next project is a4.9 in a fast back <P>After doing mine i would never put areplacement motor in when the caddy is readily availabe and EASY.<P>Later Jimmy B.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000029||rockcrawl||10-23-2001||08:48 PM|||| It seems there are four things that draw people to this swap.<BR>1. ease of installation<BR>2. low cost<BR>3. be different- never see another one parked next to you at a show<BR>4. it's a V8!!<P> I know two people who were set on doing a 4.9, and then decided to go 3800SC instead. If you want power, the 3800SC is the better choice. But it will cost more, and it doesn't have the V8 rumble. For anyone who's wondering, it sounds just like a Chevy V8.<P>Just to give you guys an idea how cheap these engines are, I've bought four of them for $50-$100 each, that includes wiring, ECM, all accesories, everything..  but I did have to pull them myself and they are as-is, condition unknown. I got a running/driving 4.9 for $350, but that included the whole car. Mileage varies from 60k to 130k miles. Every one I've opened up so far has needed nothing but a gasket set. I get full gasket sets from a guy on Ebay for $20 (auto parts gets $200). I've never seen a crank bearing that I would consider worn out, but bearings are fairly cheap so why not replace them anyway. I've passed on 4.9s for $400 because I thought it was too much. I'd rather buy an engine for $50 and spend $350 or even $600 freshening it than spend $400 on one that some guy claims is good. It's likely the 'good' engine will need the same attention. I would only take a pre-'90 engine if it was free. That's how it is around here anyway. No offense to anyone who paid more or is selling for more. I'm sure it varies with location.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Rockcrawl... how much to have you do the install on one of these anyway? <BR>BTW: are you still making the extractor hood vents? I want one of those too!<HR></BLOCKQUOTE> <BR> I'm not ready to start doing these swaps for anyone yet, there are too many uncertainties and details to work out. If I do work for someone else, I want it to be perfect. I'm still doing a lot of experimentation with mine to make it perfect. When and if the time comes, I charge an hourly labor rate that is less than half of what you would pay the GM dealer to fix your car. Again, I'm not in it to get rich, you just pay me a fair price for my time, makes no difference if I'm installing a 4.9 or replacing a manifold on your 2.8.<BR> The hood vent is $125 shipped, I have one left and that's it for a while.<P>Sorry for the long post.||||reg||1
Z||000030||FieroSoul||10-23-2001||10:35 PM||||  Don't be sorry for the long post Rockcrawl, it was good information.  I have been checking this swap out for a while and keep running into the same vacuum of info when I mention it. Thanks for sharing.<BR>  The mechanics look pretty straight forward.<BR>  The part that has me confused is the wiring.  Anyone who has tried to decipher Bubbajoes page knows what I mean.  Can you explain that to a layman?<BR>  ||||reg||6
Z||000031||rockcrawl||10-23-2001||11:00 PM||||Hey G,<BR> I tried to reply to your Email, returned twice undeliverable. You've got me all wrong! I think the pot just called the kettle black....or something like that. Please send me an alternate address so I can reply. <P>FieroSoul, it's really tough to teach someone how to rewire a car, especially over the internet. I can do it in a few hours, but to tell someone else how to do it....that's tough. Connect the green wire to the red wire....or was it blue?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"> That's where Ed's services will come into play.... shell out the cash and your on your way.||||reg||7
Z||000032||jeffndebrus||10-23-2001||11:09 PM||||I have a question.<BR>Where is the best place to buy a 4.x engine, ecm, harness ect ? junkyard- insurance company- private owner ???<BR>when will Ed Parks have his plug and play ready for this swap?||||reg||1
Z||000033||FieroSoul||10-24-2001||06:37 AM||||  Rockcrawl,<BR>  I don't want to know how to rewire a car. I just want to know why this is necessary if I have replaced the ECM with the one from the caddy and am using the caddy harness from the engine these two should connect with no need to do a pin out, right?  Or am I missing something?<BR>  Then for the wiring going forward to the instrument cluster and lights, re pin the Fiero harness into the caddy connectors or what?  <BR>   All bubbajoe says is use a meter to do a pin out.  See what I mean, somethings missing.<BR> Sorry for my long post and for maybe being a little bit slow.<P> ||||reg||6
Z||000034||cadero2dmax||10-24-2001||06:53 AM||||The two gents that installed 3.8's in lieu of a 4.9 because they thought they were getting more power absolutely like to play "my dog is bigger than your dog" with the numbers game.<P>The 3.8 has about 100 less foot pounds of torque overall than the 4.9. It has to wait for the power to come on for what seems like an eternity when compared to the V8. Although the 3.8 has better horsepower numbers - trust me - it ain't horsepower that wins the stoplight wars!!<P>In other words, the 4.9 is similar to my Cummins diesel. Gobs of torque - - RIGHT NOW - - this engine slams you into the back of your seat at the thought of pushing the loud pedal. However,also like the Cummins, the 4.9 is RPM limited. It pulls equally strong all the way to the rev limiter, which holds the motor to less than 6,000 RPM. In other words, it isn't a power motor, it is a torque motor.<P>Fiero drivers used to the 2.8 have had that same experience, how many stoplight tales of the Fiero vs. the higher powered Mustang have we heard/read about? That is what a torque motor is all about. <P>When the 3.8 starts to get into it's power band, the 4.9 has already shifted into the next gear and is back into the middle of all that torque, still pulling.<P>So, let the numbers boys scoff the 215 horsepower numbers for larger numbers on other powerplants (that do not come on until 3500 - 4000 RPM), the laugh will be on us at the next stoplight, or at the strip.<P>At the strip?? With this engine, if you can get all the 4.9 torque to the ground, your 60' times will be phenomenal compared to the 3.8. The Cad may be in top gear when it goes through the traps, but it will have to wait on the 3.8 to spool up and get through the traps in 3rd - after the motor with lesser horsepower has literally has blown the power motor away. <P>Perhaps the Challenge car will show the world what this car/engine is really capable of, but I thought Team Python had already proven it in Tucson!!<P>G ||||reg||1
Z||000035||rockcrawl||10-24-2001||07:21 AM||||Hmm, you must be running an ECM other than the stock Caddy.....or it's running in limp mode or something. The Cadillac is limited to 4500 RPM. I do agree with everything you said about the 4.9, but it sounds like you've never driven a Fiero with a supercharged 3800. Watch out for them  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Trust me, the 3800 SC driver will not be waiting on power, it's always there. In fact that's what I like more about driving the 3800, instant acceleration at any speed. The blower works wonders. And I believe the 3800 SC II has higher hp <B>and</B> torque ratings than the 4.9.||||reg||7
Z||000036||lou_dias||10-24-2001||09:24 AM||||I don't know about you guys but I'm going to try to get close to the ASG 5.0<BR>deck the heads and bump the compression to 10:1 (as opposed to the 9.5 stock or 10.5 ASG), rebuild it and overbore it to 5 liters and do some head and intake porting and polishing.<BR>I expect 250hp.  If I can get a cam or higher ration rockers, then more.||||reg||1
Z||000037||MinnGreenGT||10-24-2001||10:53 AM||||Rockcrawl... thanks for the info- it's great.  I'm going to try to convince my wife to let me order the hood vent shortly!  <P>As to the cost to install... I'm pretty sure I can handle the physical installation- guess I'm more worried about the wiring & computer stuff (I read something about someone using a Camaro ECM that's been rechipped...?)  So maybe Ed's wiring kit will solve the issue (unless you want to email me a quote to do just the wiring harness?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<P>As to G's address- my first email bounced back too... double check the reply-to address, it should be "gryan3@..." and mine origionally sent it to "gyan3@..."<P>Damn- now I'm dreaming about what I can do with my old '84!  Mmmmmm.....V8.<P>BTW: Just wondering about the name "Cadero"  is it meant to be a play on combining Caddy & Fiero, or is it actually a common name for the engine?  Just curious- I like it (would make a great custom plate on my '84   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 10-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000038||MinnGreenGT||10-24-2001||02:11 PM||||Just for informational purposes... these are the only 2 Caddy 4.X websites I've found:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/cadero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/cadero.htm</A>   (Rockcrawl's site)<BR> <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx/" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx/</A>  (BubbaJoe's site)<P>Anyone know of anymore?<P>Also, I recieved this message from Ed Parks at the FieroFactory this morning in response to a message that G forwarded for me:<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>Rob, George, and all, an 84 is not the best to start with since the C-500<BR>connection is in the center of the engine compartment along with the a/c<BR>lines, and the 84 cruise system is different.<BR> <BR>George, thats one reason we switched you to the 86. Even with "singed"<BR>wiring (battery fire), it  is at least going in the right direction (out of<BR>the way of the engine).<BR> <BR>Rob, our kits are designed to use the 4.9 injected Caddy (from 91-95<BR>Devilles) in an 85 or later Fiero body (or an 84 with 85 or later wiring<BR>harnesses, cruise control, and a/c lines). The kits come with a new, purpose<BR>built harness that simply plugs in between the Caddy computer & engine, the<BR>Fiero C-500 & C-202 connections, the Caddy relay box, and also includes an<BR>ALDL and eliminates the passkey. It also has pulleys, belt, belt tensioner,<BR>motor and trans brackets, motor & trans mounts, a/c lines, bolt on exhaust<BR>system (w/new cat) for using stock Fiero tips, new cruise vacuum supply,<BR>fuel lines, various hoses, modified thermostat housing, plugs for oil cooler<BR>lines, spacers/washers/bolts, rebuilt axles (for using Caddy 4 spd auto),<BR>and a different harness, new flywheel, new clutch, new starter, and new<BR>crossover exhaust pipe for manual cars.<BR> <BR>We are running down the last of the costs, but it still looks like an auto<BR>kit will be about $2000 and a manual kit about $1200 more.<BR> <BR>Designed to be "plug "n" play", it should require the owner to only have to<BR>change a diode for correct tach reading, provide an 87-88 4 cyl throttle<BR>cable, and a 91 Seville battery cable (unless owner wants to relocate<BR>battery to the front, but that is not necessary). The kits will have the<BR>speedometer sine wave changes built into the harnesses.<BR> <BR>It has taken a long time to develop the kits to these standards, and we are<BR>still making refinements to reduce the amount of different parts between<BR>84-87 and 88 cradles. In the meantime we have orders for 15 installations<BR>using new "crate" engines already delivered to the shop, and about the same<BR>for just the kits. Therefore, for the forseeable future, individual<BR>components will not be available.<BR> <BR>Please remember, these are not "one off" kits, they are designed to be<BR>simple/quick to install, reliable, and have ease of future service/parts<BR>availability by using mostly "off the shelf" and stock Fiero parts. They are<BR>also designed to retain the Caddy smoothness, familiar Fiero handling, and<BR>have a "factory" installation appearance.<BR> <BR>Slotting 4 cradle holes, drilling 2 others, and welding a reinforcement and<BR>2 brackets to the cradle are required. Urethane engine mounts in key areas<BR>eliminate the need for a topside dogbone. No cutting (trunk, decklid, fender<BR>well) on the Fiero is necessary.<BR> <BR>We are not/have not taken anyones $$, since the kits are not ready to "box",<BR>but will soon ask for a deposit to facilitate the mass production of the<BR>kits (as quickly as the 1st production harness is installed and proofed).<BR>Some kits should be available after the 1st of the year, but most will go<BR>for installations that are already here.<BR> <BR>George mentions 2 good sites that should allow you to "one off" your own<BR>conversion if you don't wish one of our kits.<BR> <BR>George, hope you don't mind us using your message to bring folks up to date,<BR>and hopefully cutting down the individual messages to us.<BR> <BR>Thanks.<P>Ed Parks...from: The Fiero Factory<BR>8710B Hwy 53<BR>Toney, Ala 35773<BR>Days: 256-420-5391   Nights 256-882-1568 <A HREF="http://www.welcome.to/thefierofactory" TARGET=_blank>http://www.welcome.to/thefierofactory</A> <BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The only part I question is the statement of cost... "$2000 for an auto" and "$1200 <B>more</B> for a manual"  is that right?  $3200 for a Manual Conversion kit?||||reg||1
Z||000039||mrfixit58||10-24-2001||02:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MinnGreenGT:<BR><B><BR> The only part I question is the statement of cost... "$2000 for an auto" and "$1200 [b]more</B> for a manual"  is that right?  $3200 for a Manual Conversion kit?[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I talked to Ed at the 1st Annual Florida Swap Meet about this (see what you guys missed  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ). The manual kit is an adder to the auto kit and comes with a new purpose built steel billet flywheel (NOT re-drilled) and clutch. Part of the $2000 for the auto kit (as well as the manual kit) is the new exhaust system with cat. I believe it uses the Caddy cross over pipe.<P>Personally, for that kind of money I would probably hold out for a SC3800. The main reason I like the swap is because you can do it your self... cheap... IF, you go carburetor or throttle body injection. That's not a flame or slam on Ed, that's just me. Obviously some people are willing to pay the money if he's developing the extensive plug-n-play kit.<P>Roy<P>||||reg||1
Z||000040||Daleford||10-24-2001||10:44 PM||||MinnGreen. You are right.... The name Cadero is a combination of Cadillac and Fiero.<P>Much better choice than Fillac!<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||12
